window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][5]={"university_physics_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Temperature, Equilibrium and Equations of State<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> SI units, elementary algebra, and\npressure as force per area. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the zeroth law,\ndistinguish kelvin and Celsius, and calculate ideal-gas states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State and Thermal Equilibrium<\/h2>\n<p>Thermodynamics describes macroscopic states using measurable\nquantities such as pressure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nvolume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntemperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand amount of substance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn <strong>thermal equilibrium<\/strong> two systems allowed to exchange\nonly heat have no net transfer of energy as heat on average. The zeroth\nlaw makes equilibrium transitive: if A is in thermal equilibrium with C\nand B is in thermal equilibrium with C, then A and B are in thermal\nequilibrium with each other. This is the basis for comparing states with\na thermometer. Temperature is a state variable, not the total thermal\nenergy stored in a body. A tiny piece of hot metal can have less\ninternal energy than a large volume of moderately warm water.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Temperature Scales and Gas Equation<\/h2>\n<p>Absolute temperature is expressed in kelvin. A Celsius reading\nbecomes a kelvin reading by adding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>273.15<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">273.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe size of one-degree temperature differences is equal on both scales.\nFor a sufficiently dilute gas whose particle interactions can be\nneglected within the regime studied, the ideal equation of state is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pV=nRT=Nk_{\\mathrm B}T,\\qquad R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in moles, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of particles. The gas constant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\approx8.314\\,\\mathrm{J/(mol\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a fixed amount of gas,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pV/T=\\mathrm{const}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween equilibrium states. A path in a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiagram requires further information, such as whether\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant during the change. The equation specifies states, not\nthe rate of a process.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Confined Gas<\/h2>\n<p>A closed rigid vessel contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2.00\\,\\mathrm{mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof an approximately ideal gas at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_1=1.00\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts volume is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0499<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=\\frac{nRT_1}{p_1}=\\frac{2.00\\cdot8.314\\cdot300}{10^5}\\approx0.0499\\,\\mathrm{m^3}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If it is heated to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>360<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2=360\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout changing volume or amount of gas,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_2=p_1T_2/T_1=1.20\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA temperature of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>360<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><\/mrow><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360\\,{}^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust not be substituted for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>360<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure depicts two systems in thermal contact: a common final\ntemperature is expected only after sufficient time. The actual final\ntemperature depends on their heat capacities.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_290_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two bodies at different temperatures in contact with a heat-transfer arrow\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two bodies at different temperatures in\ncontact with a heat-transfer arrow<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The zeroth law identifies equilibrium but does not calculate the\nfinal temperature.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><\/mrow><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25.0\\,{}^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto kelvin.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the pressure ratio when a rigid vessel is heated from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>250<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">250<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the zeroth law not determine the final temperature of two\nbodies placed in contact?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25.0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>273.15<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>298.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=25.0+273.15=298.15\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>250<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_2/p_1=T_2/T_1=300/250=1.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It defines the equilibrium relation; the energy balance and heat\ncapacities determine the final temperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of the Models<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal gas is a model, not a particular substance. At high density\nand near phase changes, attraction, molecular size and condensation\naffect behaviour. The van der Waals model qualitatively includes\nexcluded volume and a pressure correction, with material-dependent\nconstants. The notion of a well-defined temperature at every instant\nalso assumes sufficient local relaxation. During rapid changes or strong\nflows, a system may be out of equilibrium. Therefore its boundary,\nequilibrium approximation and fixed variables should be stated before\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pV=nRT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Temperature defines thermal equilibrium, and equations of state\nconnect measurable variables within their model assumptions. We next\nexamine a microscopic account of gases.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Kinetic Gas Theory and the\nMaxwell–Boltzmann Distribution<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-1-temperature-and-thermal-equilibrium\">University Physics 2 – 1.1 Temperature and Thermal Equilibrium<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.1, learning objectives and definition; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L1 – Temperature, Equilibrium and Equations of State · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas\">University Physics 2 – 2.1 Molecular Model of an Ideal Gas<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.1, Eq. 2.4 and pV diagrams; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L1 – Temperature, Equilibrium and Equations of State · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – Kinetic Gas Theory and the Maxwell–Boltzmann Distribution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Ideal gas equation, averages, and\nthree-dimensional velocity. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret gas pressure through\nmomentum transfer and calculate velocity characteristics of an\nequilibrium distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Microscopic Origin of Pressure<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal monatomic gas is approximated by moving particles whose\ncollisions with walls and with each other are treated as elastic. A\ncollision changes the normal component of momentum; averaging many\nimpacts produces a force and hence pressure. For isotropic velocities,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle v_x^2\\rangle=\\langle v_y^2\\rangle=\\langle v_z^2\\rangle=\\langle v^2\\rangle/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAveraging over many particles explains why macroscopic pressure appears\nsteady even though each molecule makes irregular impacts. This is an\nequilibrium model; an individual collision cannot define the pressure or\ntemperature of an entire gas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Speed Distribution<\/h2>\n<p>Momentum flux gives the pressure of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparticles of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>N<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pV=\\tfrac13Nm\\langle v^2\\rangle.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Comparison with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pV=Nk_{\\mathrm B}T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12m\\langle v^2\\rangle=\\tfrac32k_{\\mathrm B}T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn classical equilibrium the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution of speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(v)=4\\pi\\left(\\frac{m}{2\\pi k_{\\mathrm B}T}\\right)^{3/2}v^2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{mv^2}{2k_{\\mathrm B}T}\\right),\\qquad\\int_0^\\infty f(v)\\,dv=1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It describes a probability distribution, not one unique speed. At\nhigher\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe distribution broadens and shifts to faster speeds. The most probable\nspeed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{mp}}=\\sqrt{2k_{\\mathrm B}T/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile the root-mean-square speed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{3k_{\\mathrm B}T/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Characteristic Speed<\/h2>\n<p>For nitrogen molecules at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntake approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.65<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>26<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=4.65\\times10^{-26}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.380649<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>422<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>517<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{mp}}\\approx422\\,\\mathrm{m/s},\\qquad v_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\approx517\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The latter is larger because the average of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nplaces more weight on fast particles. Both characteristic speeds grow as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>T<\/mi><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, at fixed temperature, decrease with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>m<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram compares two distributions at different temperatures for the\nsame molecular mass. Both enclosed areas are one; a higher, narrower\ncurve does not mean that more molecules are present.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_269_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two normalised speed distributions of the same molecules at 300 and 600 kelvin\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two normalised speed distributions of the\nsame molecules at 300 and 600 kelvin<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The hotter distribution is broader and centred on higher\nspeeds.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{rms}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis quadrupled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{rms}}/v_{\\mathrm{mp}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a classical gas.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the width of the speed distribution matter at higher\ntemperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msqrt><mi>T<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\propto\\sqrt T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.225<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{rms}}/v_{\\mathrm{mp}}=\\sqrt{3/2}\\approx1.225<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Temperature characterises a spread of speeds; transport and\ncollision rates depend on that distribution, not merely its peak.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Range and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Maxwell–Boltzmann statistics assumes classical, nondegenerate\nparticles in thermal equilibrium. Electrons in metals and ultracold\nquantum gases require different occupation statistics. Molecules may\nstore energy in rotational and vibrational modes; the relation for\n<strong>translational<\/strong> kinetic energy remains useful, but the\nfull internal energy is then not simply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac32Nk_{\\mathrm B}T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRapid nozzle flows and strong gradients can also produce anisotropic,\nnonthermal distributions. Connecting microscopic motion to temperature\ntherefore depends on a justified equilibrium approximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Macroscopic gas pressure is an average of microscopic momentum\ntransfers. Heat and work next connect microscopic energy with\nmacroscopic balances.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">First Law, Heat and Volume\nWork<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-summary\">University Physics 2 – Chapter 2 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.1, §2.4; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L2 – Kinetic Gas Theory and the Maxwell–Boltzmann Distribution · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – First Law, Heat and Volume Work<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Ideal gas equation of state and\nelementary mechanics. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Balance energy transfer as heat\nand work and distinguish isochoric and isobaric processes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>System Boundary and Signs<\/h2>\n<p>Heat and work are ways of transferring energy across a system\nboundary; they are not substances stored inside a body. <strong>Internal\nenergy<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nby contrast, is a state variable. For a closed system with no change in\nmacroscopic kinetic or potential energy choose the sign convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor heat supplied to the system and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor work done by the system on its surroundings. Energy conservation\nthen reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=Q-W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOther textbooks take work done on the system as positive, so the sign\nconvention must be explicit. Adiabatic means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot necessarily constant temperature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy Balance and Process Paths<\/h2>\n<p>For a quasistatic expansion against the system pressure, the\nvolume-work increment is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta W=p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand along a path\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=\\int_{V_1}^{V_2}p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA real irreversible change normally requires the actual external\npressure at the moving boundary. For an ideal gas with fixed amount,\ninternal energy depends only on temperature; when heat capacity is\nconstant,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=nC_V\\Delta T,\\qquad Q=\\Delta U+W.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At constant volume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dV=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_V=\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt constant pressure for an ideal gas,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=p\\Delta V=nR\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_p=n(C_V+R)\\Delta T=nC_p\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_p-C_V=R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds for molar ideal-gas heat capacities. In a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiagram, the area below a quasistatic path represents its volume\nwork.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Constant-Pressure Heating<\/h2>\n<p>A sample of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1.00\\,\\mathrm{mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof an ideal monatomic gas is heated at constant pressure by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=100\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_V=\\tfrac32R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_p=\\tfrac52R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1247<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>831<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2079<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=\\tfrac32nR\\Delta T\\approx1247\\,\\mathrm J,\\qquad W=nR\\Delta T\\approx831\\,\\mathrm J,\\qquad Q\\approx2079\\,\\mathrm J.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The sum of internal-energy change and work done agrees with the heat\nsupplied. For the same temperature increase at constant volume the model\nneeds only about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1247<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1247\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof heat. The figure shows an isobaric path as a horizontal line and the\nrectangle representing work. Constant heat capacities and only three\ntranslational degrees of freedom are assumed.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_099_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Pressure-volume diagram with an isobaric path and shaded volume work\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Pressure-volume diagram with an isobaric\npath and shaded volume work<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>For a quasistatic isobaric path, work equals pressure times\nvolume change.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A closed system receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=500\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand performs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=180\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For an isothermal state change of an ideal gas,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat follows about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Explain why the area below a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncurve only gives actual work with the appropriate pressure\ndefinition.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>320<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=Q-W=320\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With our convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhether or not the path is reversible; the numerical amounts remain path\ndependent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">During irreversible expansion, internal pressure can differ from the\nexternal boundary pressure, which determines the mechanical work.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State and Process Quantities<\/h2>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepend on the state, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepend on the path. One may therefore define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween states but not a unique “heat contained in a state.” Free\nexpansion of an ideal gas into vacuum does no boundary work if the\nexternal pressure vanishes; despite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit can have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOpen flowing systems additionally require enthalpy, flow work and\nstreams of kinetic energy. The simple closed-system balance does not\nreplace a general control-volume calculation. Efficiency claims also\nrequire the second law.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The first law accounts for energy but does not select a preferred\nprocess direction. The second law introduces entropy and\nirreversibility.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Entropy, Second Law and\nIrreversibility<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-2-work-heat-and-internal-energy\">University Physics 2 – 3.2 Work, Heat, and Internal Energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.2, Work Done by a System; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L3 – First Law, Heat and Volume Work · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-summary\">University Physics 2 – Chapter 3 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.2–3.4; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L3 – First Law, Heat and Volume Work · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M5-L4 – Entropy, Second Law and Irreversibility<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> First law, ideal gas and logarithmic\nintegration. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate reversible entropy\nchanges and explain irreversible entropy production.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Direction of Spontaneous Processes<\/h2>\n<p>Conservation of energy alone allows many hypothetical process\ndirections. Gas initially confined to one side of an isolated vessel\nspontaneously fills the available space; the macroscopic reverse does\nnot occur although the total energy may be unchanged. The <strong>second\nlaw<\/strong> selects a direction: the entropy of an isolated total\nsystem increases for real irreversible changes and remains constant in\nthe ideal reversible limit. “Disorder” can be an analogy but is not a\nprecise definition. Entropy is a state variable measured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA thermodynamic entropy balance distinguishes entropy transported across\nthe boundary from entropy produced internally.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Entropy as a State Function<\/h2>\n<p>The entropy difference between equilibrium states can be computed\nusing a hypothetical reversible reference path:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dS=\\frac{\\delta Q_{\\mathrm{rev}}}{T},\\qquad\\Delta S=\\int_1^2\\frac{\\delta Q_{\\mathrm{rev}}}{T}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This equation must not be applied to arbitrary irreversible heat\ntransfer after dropping the “rev” subscript. For an ideal gas with\nconstant heat capacity,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S=nC_V\\ln\\frac{T_2}{T_1}+nR\\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Starting with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dU=nC_VdT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta Q_{\\mathrm{rev}}=dU+p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ninsert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=nRT/V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand integrate. For an isolated total system without entropy transfer,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S=S_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReversible and irreversible paths may join the same end states; the\nstate change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis then identical.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Free Expansion<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal gas of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1.00\\,\\mathrm{mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpands freely and in isolation from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_2=2V_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the vacuum experiment\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximately, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2=T_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal gas. The actual expansion is irreversible, but the entropy\ndifference can be evaluated along a <strong>hypothetical reversible\nisothermal path<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.76<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S=nR\\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1}=8.314\\ln2\\approx5.76\\,\\mathrm{J/K}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The entropy increase was not carried in by heat: it was generated by\nirreversibility. The illustration separates the suddenly opened\npartition from the computational comparison between states. Real gases\ncan show small temperature changes because their internal energy is not\nonly a function of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_083_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Enclosed chamber before and after removing a partition with representative gas molecules\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Enclosed chamber before and after\nremoving a partition with representative gas molecules<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the ideal-gas model temperature stays constant while\naccessible volume and entropy increase.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor free doubling of volume of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.00\\,\\mathrm{mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof ideal gas with the same final temperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can a refrigerator reduce entropy in its interior?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S=Q/T=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninvalid for free expansion?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11.52<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S=2R\\ln2\\approx11.52\\,\\mathrm{J/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes; it exports heat and entropy, while the total entropy including\nthe surroundings does not decrease.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dS=\\delta Q_{\\mathrm{rev}}/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a reversible-path relation, not the entropy change along the actual\nirreversible expansion.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local Production and Caveats<\/h2>\n<p>Conduction between bodies at different temperatures, friction and\nmixing produce entropy. Strongly nonuniform or rapidly evolving states\nshould not be assigned equilibrium variables uncritically; a\n<strong>local<\/strong> entropy balance is often used when local thermal\nequilibrium is a justified approximation. A subsystem can export entropy\nand therefore show a negative entropy change; the second law concerns\nthe system <strong>together with its surroundings<\/strong>, not\nnecessarily each subsystem separately. Microscopic equations that are\nreversible in time do not contradict macroscopic entropy growth:\nstatistical initial conditions and the huge number of microscopic states\nmatter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Entropy is a well-defined state variable; nonnegative entropy\nproduction characterises irreversible operation. This direction\nconstraint limits heat engines.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Heat Engines, the Carnot Limit\nand Refrigerators<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-1-reversible-and-irreversible-processes\">University Physics 2 – 4.1 Reversible and Irreversible Processes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.1, free-expansion example; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L4 – Entropy, Second Law and Irreversibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-6-entropy\">University Physics 2 – 4.6 Entropy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.6, Eq. 4.8; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L4 – Entropy, Second Law and Irreversibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M5-L5 – Heat Engines, the Carnot Limit and Refrigerators<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> First and second laws; temperature in\nkelvin. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Set up cyclic energy balances\nand calculate ideal efficiency and coefficient of performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cycle and Energy Balance<\/h2>\n<p>A heat engine draws heat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom a hot reservoir, rejects heat of magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_c&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto a cooler one and delivers work\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover a cycle. After one period the working substance has returned to its\noriginal state, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U_{\\mathrm{cyc}}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Q_h-Q_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the thermal efficiency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=W/Q_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese values are magnitudes of exchanged energies; the heat leaving the\nworking substance is negative in its signed energy balance. A cycle may\nappear as a closed loop on a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nplot, and its oriented enclosed area equals net work in the quasistatic\nlimit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Carnot Limit and Reverse Operation<\/h2>\n<p>For a reversible cycle exchanging heat with two reservoirs, entropy\nbalance gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h/T_h=Q_c/T_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing absolute temperatures. The greatest possible engine efficiency\nbetween the reservoirs is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>η<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mathrm C}=1-\\frac{T_c}{T_h},\\qquad \\eta\\leq\\eta_{\\mathrm C}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In reverse operation, work must be supplied to transport heat from a\ncooler region to a warmer one. For a refrigerator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{COP}_R=Q_c/W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a heat pump\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{COP}_{HP}=Q_h/W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReversible upper bounds are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{COP}_{R,\\mathrm C}=\\frac{T_c}{T_h-T_c},\\qquad\\mathrm{COP}_{HP,\\mathrm C}=\\frac{T_h}{T_h-T_c}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A coefficient of performance exceeding one does not violate\nconservation of energy: some delivered heat comes from a reservoir.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Engine and Refrigerator<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal engine runs between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_h=600\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_c=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h=1000\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper cycle. The reversible limit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>600<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mathrm C}=1-300/600=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=500\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_c=500\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napply at that limit. An ideal refrigerator between the same temperatures\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{COP}_{R,\\mathrm C}=300/(600-300)=1.00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nremoving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the cold side requires at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof work. The illustration separates heat transfers from the single work\ntransfer and uses temperatures only in kelvin.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_126_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Hot reservoir, engine, cold reservoir and directed heat and work arrows\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Hot reservoir, engine, cold reservoir and\ndirected heat and work arrows<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Work output is the difference between absorbed and rejected\nheat.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mathrm C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_h=500\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_c=250\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An engine receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1200\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand rejects\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>780<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">780\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate work and efficiency.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which quantity is maximised by an ideal heat pump:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W/Q_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_h/W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>250<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>500<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mathrm C}=1-250/500=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>420<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=420\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>420<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1200<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=420/1200=0.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The useful coefficient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{COP}_{HP}=Q_h/W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot its reciprocal.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Real Machines Differ<\/h2>\n<p>Friction, finite temperature differences during heat transfer,\nthrottling losses and incomplete expansion generate entropy.\nConsequently real heat engines operate below the Carnot limit for fixed\nreservoir temperatures. The Carnot expression says nothing about power\noutput, size, material constraints or cost. A refrigerator working over\na small temperature difference can have a high theoretical performance\ncoefficient, but it may require large heat exchangers or long transfer\ntimes to move heat across a small gradient. Quantitative design also\nneeds mass flow, pressure drops and real material properties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Energy conservation gives net work; the second law limits the usable\nfraction. The next topic expresses equilibrium constraints using\nthermodynamic potentials.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Thermodynamic Potentials and\nMaxwell Relations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-summary\">University Physics 2 – Chapter 4 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.2–4.3; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L5 – Heat Engines, the Carnot Limit and Refrigerators · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M5-L6 – Thermodynamic Potentials and Maxwell Relations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Partial derivatives, differentials,\nfirst and second laws. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain Legendre transforms and\ncorrectly derive two Maxwell relations from state functions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Natural State Variables<\/h2>\n<p>For a simple homogeneous compressible closed system of fixed particle\nnumber with only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwork, the thermodynamic fundamental relation for reversible changes is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dU=T\\,dS-p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus entropy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand volume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare natural variables of internal energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(S,V)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe minus sign in front of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows from the chosen first-law work convention. In experiments,\ntemperature and pressure are often easier to hold constant than entropy\nand volume. A Legendre transform replaces an independent variable by its\nconjugate derivative. It changes the description of a state without\nchanging the underlying physical system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Potentials by Changing Variables<\/h2>\n<p>Define enthalpy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=U+pV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nHelmholtz energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=U-TS<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand Gibbs energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=U+pV-TS<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt fixed particle number,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>d<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>d<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>d<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\ndU&amp;=T\\,dS-p\\,dV,\\\\\ndH&amp;=T\\,dS+V\\,dp,\\\\\ndF&amp;=-S\\,dT-p\\,dV,\\\\\ndG&amp;=-S\\,dT+V\\,dp.\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dF=dU-T\\,dS-S\\,dT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dF=-S\\,dT-p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince mixed second derivatives of smooth state functions agree, Maxwell\nrelations follow, including<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left(\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial V}\\right)_T=\\left(\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial T}\\right)_V,\\qquad\\left(\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial p}\\right)_T=-\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial T}\\right)_p.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The first follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(T,V)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the second from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(T,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Ideal Gas Response<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal gas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=nRT/V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence the right side of the first Maxwell relation at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nR/V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe earlier entropy change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta S=nC_V\\ln(T_2/T_1)+nR\\ln(V_2/V_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>V<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left(\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial V}\\right)_T=\\frac{nR}{V}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial T}\\right)_V.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second relation can also be tested:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=nRT/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-(\\partial V/\\partial T)_p=-nR/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn variables\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T,p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe entropy formula likewise gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\partial S/\\partial p)_T=-nR/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration maps each of the four potentials to its natural\nvariables and differential terms.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_223_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four boxes for internal energy, enthalpy, Helmholtz and Gibbs energy with transformation arrows\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four boxes for internal energy, enthalpy,\nHelmholtz and Gibbs energy with transformation arrows<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Legendre transforms exchange natural variables while describing\nthe same equilibrium physics.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State the natural variables of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dF=-S\\,dT-p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=U-TS<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\partial p/\\partial T)_V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal gas with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2.00\\,\\mathrm{mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.050<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0.050\\,\\mathrm{m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(S,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(T,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dF=dU-T\\,dS-S\\,dT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nsubstitute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dU=T\\,dS-p\\,dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto cancel the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\,dS<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nterms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.050<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>333<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nR/V=2\\cdot8.314/0.050\\approx333\\,\\mathrm{Pa/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and Stability<\/h2>\n<p>If particle number varies, the chemical-potential term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu\\,dN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nenters; mixtures require a sum over components. Additional work modes,\nsuch as magnetic polarisation, change the independent variables and the\nrelevant Legendre transform. Maxwell relations apply locally within\nsmooth phase regions; first-order phase transitions may introduce\ndiscontinuities in derivatives. Under appropriate boundary conditions,\nequilibrium minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T,V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T,p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese claims require matching reservoirs and allowed processes; saying\n“energy always becomes minimal” without qualification would be\nincorrect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Thermodynamic potentials associate equilibrium conditions with\nsuitable control variables. Their derivatives enable indirect\nmeasurements and underpin phase equilibrium.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Phase Changes, Latent Heat and\nPhase Equilibrium<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Arovas%29/02%3A_Thermodynamics/2.08%3A_Maxwell_Relations\">LibreTexts – 2.8 Maxwell Relations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 1357–1367, E/F/H/G; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L6 – Thermodynamic Potentials and Maxwell Relations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M5-L7 – Phase Changes, Latent Heat and Phase Equilibrium<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Gibbs energy, entropy change and the\nfirst law. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Read phase diagrams and derive\nthe relation between coexistence curves and latent heat.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equilibrium Between Phases<\/h2>\n<p>A pure substance may exist as solid, liquid or gas at different\ncombinations of pressure and temperature. Along a <strong>coexistence\ncurve<\/strong>, two phases are in equilibrium at the same temperature\nand pressure; their molar Gibbs energies, or chemical potentials, are\nequal. A phase diagram maps stable equilibrium states; it is not\nautomatically a time history of the transformation. A triple point is a\nspecific pair of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat which three phases coexist. During a first-order transition at fixed\npressure, temperature stays constant in the idealised quasistatic\nequilibrium limit even while energy is transferred.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Latent Heat and Coexistence Slope<\/h2>\n<p>For a first-order transition the molar latent heat supplied at\nconstant pressure is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=T\\Delta s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=s_\\beta-s_\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the molar entropy difference. Along the coexistence curve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\alpha(T,p)=g_\\beta(T,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dg=-s\\,dT+v\\,dp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso differentiation gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0=-\\Delta s\\,dT+\\Delta v\\,dp,\\qquad\\frac{dp}{dT}=\\frac{L}{T\\Delta v}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is the Clapeyron equation.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta v=v_\\beta-v_\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be a molar volume difference consistent with molar\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe familiar approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\ln p/dT\\approx L/(RT^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor vaporisation also assumes ideal vapour and negligible liquid\nvolume.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Water Vaporisation<\/h2>\n<p>For water near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><\/mrow><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,{}^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand standard pressure, use illustrative values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>40.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\approx40.7\\,\\mathrm{kJ/mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>373<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\approx373\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta v\\approx0.030\\,\\mathrm{m^3/mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor liquid to vapour. Clapeyron’s equation then gives approximately<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>700<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>373<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.64<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dp}{dT}\\approx\\frac{40\\,700}{373\\cdot0.030}\\approx3.64\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{Pa/K}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This local estimate applies over a narrow temperature interval only.\nAccurate calculations require vapour tables and temperature-dependent\nphase properties. The illustration is a schematic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphase diagram with a triple and a critical point; it is explicitly not\nto scale. Unlike many substances, water has a negatively sloping\nsolid–liquid coexistence line in the usual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresentation because of its density anomaly.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_207_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic pressure-temperature phase map for water with a negatively sloped melting curve\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic pressure-temperature phase map\nfor water with a negatively sloped melting curve<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The sketch is not to scale; the melting curve for water slopes to\nthe left.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does equality of the molar Gibbs energies of two phases\nmean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=40.7\\,\\mathrm{kJ/mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>373<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=373\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dp/dT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor melting of a material with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta v&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The phases may coexist in thermodynamic equilibrium.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>109<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=L/T\\approx109\\,\\mathrm{J/(mol\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Clapeyron gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dp/dT&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nas for the water melting curve in the relevant region.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Critical Point and Model Limits<\/h2>\n<p>Real materials may show nucleation barriers, supersaturation and\nmetastable states that are not captured by a simple equilibrium map. The\nliquid–gas coexistence curve terminates at the critical point; beyond\nit, a sharp distinction between liquid and gas disappears. Models such\nas van der Waals display qualitative coexistence but are often\nquantitatively poor near real critical points. Latent heat must be\nspecified either per unit mass in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor per mole in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/mol}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nconfusing the two produces incorrect numbers. A flat transition\ntemperature is conditional on fixed external pressure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Phase boundaries link entropy change, transformation energy and\nvolume difference. The next lesson addresses how thermal energy travels\nthrough space.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Conduction, Convection and\nThermal Radiation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes\">University Physics 2 – 1.5 Phase Changes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.5, Anfang; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L7 – Phase Changes, Latent Heat and Phase Equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas\">University Physics 2 – 2.1 Molecular Model of an Ideal Gas<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.1, Eq. 2.4 and pV diagrams; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L7 – Phase Changes, Latent Heat and Phase Equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M5-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M5-L8 – Conduction, Convection and Thermal Radiation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Heat and Thermodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Temperature gradient, energy\nconservation and elementary geometry. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish heat-transfer\nmechanisms and calculate simple conduction and radiation balances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three Heat Transfer Mechanisms<\/h2>\n<p>Energy can be transferred as heat by microscopic exchange within\nmaterial (<strong>conduction<\/strong>), by moving fluids\n(<strong>convection<\/strong>) or by electromagnetic emission and\nabsorption (<strong>radiation<\/strong>). Heat is transferred energy,\nwhereas temperature describes the local thermodynamic state. In ordinary\nconduction, temperature gradients drive thermal energy from hot to cold.\nConvection combines fluid motion with near-wall regions where conduction\nis significant. Thermal radiation does not require a material medium, so\nit also operates in vacuum. Several mechanisms commonly act together in\ndevices; separating them is primarily a modelling choice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local and Global Heat Balances<\/h2>\n<p>Fourier’s law for isotropic media writes heat-flux density as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol q=-\\lambda\\nabla T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere thermal conductivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{W/(m\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor stationary one-dimensional conduction through a plane layer of\nthickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout internal sources,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q_{\\mathrm{cond}}=\\lambda A\\frac{T_h-T_c}{d}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For an idealised grey surface with emissivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninside a large uniform-temperature environment, the Stefan–Boltzmann law\ngives net radiative power<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.670374419<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">4<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q_{\\mathrm{rad}}=\\varepsilon\\sigma A(T_s^4-T_{\\mathrm{sur}}^4),\\qquad\\sigma\\approx5.670374419\\times10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm{W/(m^2\\,K^4)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An empirical model for free or forced convection at a surface is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q_{\\mathrm{conv}}=hA(T_s-T_f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCoefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on shape, flow and material properties rather than being a\nuniversal material constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Wall and Radiation<\/h2>\n<p>A homogeneous wall with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.80\\,\\mathrm{W/(m\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseparates surfaces at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>310<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_h=310\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>290<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_c=290\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts steady conduction power is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>310<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>290<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>320<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q_{\\mathrm{cond}}=0.80\\cdot2.0\\cdot(310-290)/0.10=320\\,\\mathrm W.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Separately, an ideal black surface with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1.0\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>310<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s=310\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nradiating to surroundings at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>290<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{\\mathrm{sur}}=290\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nloses roughly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>123<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">123\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnet. These are <strong>two different model setups<\/strong>: the powers\nshould not be added into a single wall model without coupling boundary\nconditions and areas. The graphic separately depicts conduction through\na plate, heat removal by fluid flow and radiation arrows.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_128_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three side-by-side technical schematics of conduction, convection and radiation\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three side-by-side technical schematics\nof conduction, convection and radiation<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Conduction requires a thermal gradient, convection fluid motion\nand radiation no medium.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q_{\\mathrm{cond}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled with all other quantities unchanged?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give the units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is it incorrect to insert Celsius readings in the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nradiation law?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Steady conduction power is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\lambda]=\\mathrm{W/(m\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[h]=\\mathrm{W/(m^2\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Stefan–Boltzmann uses absolute thermodynamic temperature; the fourth\npower is not invariant under a shifted temperature scale.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limits and Coupling<\/h2>\n<p>Fourier’s linear relation is effective for many continua, but\nnanoscale paths, strong anisotropy and temperature-dependent\nconductivities may require extended models. Surface temperatures alone\nmay not determine the full temperature profile: contact resistance and\nadditional layers matter. The radiation expression assumes a suitable\nenvironment and an approximation of diffuse grey emission; surfaces\nfacing one another require view factors. The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndependence always uses kelvin and applies to <strong>absolute<\/strong>\ntemperatures. In coupled problems, the energy balance must hold at each\ninterface, or the transfer model becomes inconsistent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Conduction, convection and radiation require different material and\nboundary models. Complex thermal systems need a coupled energy balance\nacross these mechanisms.<\/p>\n<p>The next module builds on these principles to explore other fields of\nphysics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-summary\">University Physics 2 – Chapter 1 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.6, lines 54–59; Subject relevance to lesson: M5-L8 – Conduction, Convection and Thermal Radiation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
